d from others\u2019 tiles. The chow [B4,B5,B6] was formed by Chi B5 \u2014 which means someone discarded B5, and I used it \u2014 so that meld is also exposed and melded.\n- Therefore, **all my melds are exposed**, formed from other players\u2019 discards.\n- If I win by claiming a discard (not self-draw), I qualify for **Melded Hand** (6 pts).\n- Additionally, I may get **All Chows**? No \u2014 I have two pungs and one chow.\n- Or **Outside Hand**? Only if every meld contains terminals or honors. Let's check:\n  - [F1,F1,F1]: honor \u2192 OK\n  - [F3,F3,F3]: honor \u2192 OK\n  - [B4,B5,B6]: middle simples \u2192 not terminal/honor \u2192 disqualifies **Outside Hand**\nSo not valid.\n\nBest bet: aim for **All Pungs** (6 pts) + **Prevalent Wind** (2) + **Seat Wind** (2) = 10 pts \u2014 but I don\u2019t have all pungs.\n\nAlternatively, try to reach **Half Flush**? Not likely \u2014 my hand is mixed.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps I already have enough if I complete a simple wait?\n\nBut I must evaluate what would make me win.\n\nCurrently, my hand is not complete \u2014 I have 5 loose tiles. I need to discard one to keep 4, aiming to draw or claim the winning tile.\n\nBut none of my current singles form an immediate pair or set.\n\nWait \u2014 this suggests I'm one tile away from completing a pair after discarding one.\n\nYes! After discarding one tile, I will have four tiles in hand. Then, when I draw or claim a tile that pairs with one of them, I win.\n\nSo I must choose which tile to discard now to maximize safety and chance to complete a legal hand.\n\nLooking again:\n\nHand: W2, W4, W5, B5, J1\n\nPossible useful combinations:\n- W4 + W5 + ? \u2192 could wait on W3 or W6 for chow, but W3 only 3 left, already discarded by next player (Play W3), and W6 discarded twice \u2014 possible depletion.\n- B5: only one left \u2014 very hard to get.\n- J1: one in hand, 4 unshown \u2014 actually, 4 copies total, none seen yet? Wait: unshown says J1:4 \u2014 meaning all four still available? But I have one \u2014 so 3 remain.\n\nWait: \"Unshown tiles are\" list quantities remaining in wall and discard pools \u2014 i.e., not in hands or melds.\n\nI have one J1, unshown says J1:4 \u2014 contradiction?\n\nNo: \"Unshown\" likely means *remaining unseen*, including wall and dead pile \u2014 so total copies are 4. If unshown says J1:4, and I hold one, then total 5? Impossible.\n\nAh \u2014 correction: there are only 4 copies of each tile.\n\nTherefore, \u201cUnshown\u201d = number of that tile *not visible in melds or discards*. It does not account for what players hold.\n\nSo actual remaining = Unshown value.\n\nFor example, if I hold one J1, and unshown says J1:4, that means no J1 has been discarded or exposed \u2014 so 3 remain in wall/discard pool.\n\nSimilarly, F1: unshown=1, but I have a pung [F1,F1,F1] \u2192 that\u2019s 3, so only one F1 left \u2014 matches unshown=1.\n\nSo yes, unshown reflects remaining tiles not yet revealed.\n\nTherefore:\n- J1: unshown=4 \u2192 4 copies exist, none revealed yet \u2192 I hold one \u2192 3 left in play.\n- B5: unshown=1 \u2192 only one copy left in total (not counting mine). I used one in chow [B4,B5,B6], so that\u2019s one. I now hold one \u2192 total two accounted for. There are four total \u2192 so two should be out. But unshown=1 \u2192 implies only one remains unseen \u2014 possible one was discarded.\n\nIndeed, previous player played B5, and others too \u2014 multiple B5 discards.\n\nSo only one B5 left \u2014 very dangerous to wait on.\n\nSame with T9: unshown=1, but opponent has pung [T9,T9,T9] \u2192 3 used, unshown=1 \u2192 one left.\n\nNow back to decision.\n\nI must discard one of: J1, W4, W5, B5, W2.\n\nLet\u2019s assess risk and utility.\n\nOption analysis:\n\n1. **Play B5**: \n   - Risk: Only one B5 left. Others have discarded it freely \u2014 likely safe.\n   - But I already used one in chow, holding another \u2014 discarding it breaks any hope of pair.\n   - However, pairing B5 is unlikely due to scarcity.\n   - Also, B5 is a middle tile, not useful for honors-based hands.\n   - But if I keep it, and later draw another, I could form a pair \u2014 but only one left \u2192 1/remaining tiles chance.\n   - Verdict: low value, relatively safe to discard.\n\n2. **Play W2 / W4 / W5**:\n   - All are Character suit tiles.\n   - W2: unshown=4 \u2192 all copies available \u2192 safe?\n     - But next player discarded W3, W6 \u2014 possibly building middle Characters?\n     - Across player discarded W5, W6, W8 \u2014 same trend.\n     - So they might be collecting middle W tiles.\n     - Holding W2, W4, W5 \u2014 could go for [W2,W3,W4], [W4,W5,W6], etc.\n     - But W3 unshown=3 \u2014 down from 4, someone discarded one.\n     - Next player played W3 \u2014 so maybe doesn't want it.\n     - Still, possibility exists.\n   - If I keep W4 and W5, I could wait on W3 or W6 to form chow.\n   - But better to keep two connected than isolated ones.\n\n3. **Play W2**:\n   - Least connected \u2014 W2 only helps [W1,W2,W3] or [W2,W3,W4].\n   - W1 unshown=4 \u2192 full supply, but no sign of anyone collecting low W.\n   - Discarding W2 is safest among W tiles \u2014 least likely to complete opponent\u2019s hand.\n\n4. **Play J1**:\n   - Red Dragon \u2014 no one has shown it.\n   - I have one, unshown=4 \u2192 so none revealed \u2014 safe?\n   - But dragons are often kept for pungs.\n   - However, no one has Peng\u2019d J1 \u2014 so probably not building.\n   - Discarding J1 is moderately safe.\n\nBut consider scoring.\n\nIf I win with current structure:\n- Melds: [F1x3], [B4,B5,B6], [F3x3]\n- Need two more melds or a pair and three melds.\n\nWait \u2014 no: standard hand is four melds and a pair? No \u2014 four melds (or sets) and a pair \u2014 total 14 tiles.\n\nMy current state:\n- Melds: 3 sets \u00d7 3 tiles = 9 tiles\n- Hand: 5 tiles\n\u2192 Total: 14 tiles \u2192 I must discard one to go to 13, then draw/claim one to win.\n\nAfter discarding one, I'll have 4 tiles in hand. To win, the new tile must form a pair with one existing, or complete a chow/pung.\n\nPossible waits:\n- Pair wait: if I keep four distinct tiles, I need one of them to pair up.\n- Chow wait: e.g., keep W4,W5 \u2192 wait on W3/W6\n- But I can't form new meld without drawing.\n\nSo most likely win is by making a pair.\n\nBest strategy: discard the least useful tile, keep ones that can form sequences.\n\nBest keep: W4 and W5 \u2014 together they can form part of [W3,W4,W5], [W4,W5,W6]\n\nAlso keep B5? But only one left.\n\nKeep J1? Standalone.\n\nSo ideal keep: W4, W5, and either B5 or J1 \u2014 but must discard two? No \u2014 only one action: discard one now.\n\nValid actions are all \"Play X\" \u2014 so I must choose one tile to discard.\n\nThen later, I may draw and win.\n\nSo I should discard the tile that is:\n- Least likely to help me complete a hand\n- Safest (least likely to feed opponent)\n\nAmong W2, W4, W5, B5, J1 \u2014 W2 is weakest.\n\nIt doesn't connect with W4/W5 like W3 would.\n\nW2+W4+W5 cannot form a chow.\n\nOnly way W2 helps is [W1,W2,W3] or [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 requires W3, which is somewhat rare.\n\nWhereas W4+W5 can wait on W3 or W6 for two-way wait.\n\nB5 is singleton, only one left.\n\nJ1 is dragon \u2014 could pair, 3 left.\n\nBut dragon pung gives 2 pts (Dragon Pung), and if I get a pair, it's just a pair.\n\nBut I already have two wind pungs \u2014 adding a dragon pung would give **Two Dragon Pungs? No \u2014 dragons are J1/J2/J3; F1/F2/F3/F4 are winds.\n\nSo F1 and F3 are both winds \u2014 not dragons.\n\nSo I have two wind pungs \u2014 but pattern is \"Two Dragon Pungs\", not \"Two Wind Pungs\".\n\nThere is no scoring pattern for multiple wind pungs except Big/Little Four Winds.\n\nSo only scoring from F1: Prevalent Wind (2) and Seat Wind (2).\n\nF3: just a wind pung \u2014 no point unless part of Big/Little Four Winds.\n\nSo no extra point for F3.\n\nBack.\n\nTo maximize chance to win, I should keep connected tiles.\n\nSo best to keep W4 and W5.\n\nThus, discard W2.\n\nOther options:\n- Discard B5: but then lose chance at pair; and B5 is scarce.\n- Discard J1: lose dragon pair chance.\n- But W2 is less valuable than either.\n\nMoreover, W2 is a terminal \u2014 if I keep it, and win with it in pair, it contributes to **Pung of Terminals or Honors**? No \u2014 it's a pair, not pung.\n\nBut having a terminal in hand doesn't score.\n\nOnly if I had a pung of W1/W9/B1/etc.\n\nSo no.\n\nFinal choice: **Play W2**\n\nBecause:\n- W2 is the least connected tile in my hand.\n- W4 and W5 offer a potential two-sided wait ([W3,W4,W5] or [W4,W5,W6]) if I draw W3 or W6.\n- B5 and J1 are standalone but can form pairs (though B5 is scarce).\n- Discarding W2 maximizes future flexibility.\n\nAnswer format:\n\nSituation: I have three melds (two wind pungs and one chow) and five loose tiles: W2, W4, W5, B5, J1. I need to discard one to maintain a 13-tile hand. My goal is to complete a legal 8-point hand, likely relying on Melded Hand (6 pts) + Prevalent Wind (2 pts) + Seat Wind (2 pts) = 10 pts if I win by discard.\nEvaluation: Keeping W4 and W5 offers potential for a two-sided chow wait (W3 or W6). B5 is scarce (only one left), but holding it allows a slim pair chance. J1 has three copies left and could form a dragon pair. W2 is isolated and contributes little to connectivity.\nReason: Discarding W2 preserves the connected W4-W5 pair, increasing chances of forming a chow later, while sacrificing the least useful tile.\nAnswer: Play W2"}
