st **discard one**, and hope to draw the other to pair.\n\nWait possibilities:\n- If I discard **B6**, I will be waiting on **W3** to pair (need another W3). There are **2 W3 tiles unshown**, so possible.\n- If I discard **W3**, I will be waiting on **B6** to pair. Only **1 B6 tile remains unshown** (since one is in my hand, one in next player's chow [B6,B7,B8], and one in [B4,B5,B6]\u2014wait, mine is from a chi, so total: 4 copies. Let's count:\n  - Total B6: 4 copies\n  - In use: \n    - My chow [B4,B5,B6] \u2192 1\n    - Next player's chow [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 1\n    - Across player's [B7,B8,B9] doesn't use B6\n    - Previous player discarded B6? No, he played B8, B5, B9, etc. No B6 discard seen.\n    - Discard history: I don\u2019t see B6 discarded elsewhere. So likely 2 B6 still out.\n    But unshown says B6: 2 \u2192 yes, so 2 left.\n\nWait correction: Unshown says B6: 2 \u2192 so 2 copies remain in wall or undrawn.\n\nSo both waits are viable:\n- Wait for W3 (2 left) after discarding B6\n- Wait for B6 (2 left) after discarding W3\n\nNow scoring potential:\n\nCurrent hand structure: Four melds + pair. All exposed except the chow [B4,B5,B6] was formed by Chi \u2192 so it's exposed. All my melds are exposed \u2192 **Melded Hand** (6 points) applies.\n\nAlso:\n- Pung of F1 (East Wind) \u2014 same as Prevailing Wind and Seat Wind \u2192 **Prevalent Wind (2)** and **Seat Wind (2)**\n- Pung of F3 (West Wind) \u2014 not prevailing or seat \u2192 no bonus\n- No honor pair, but F1 is counted.\n\nOther patterns:\n- **All Pungs**? No \u2014 I have a chow \u2192 invalid.\n- **All Chows**? No \u2014 I have pungs/kongs \u2192 invalid.\n- **Half Flush**? My melds include W and B suits and honors \u2192 mixed \u2192 no.\n- **One Voided Suit**? I have W, B, and honors \u2192 missing T? Wait: I have no Bamboo? Wait: B4,B5,B6 is Bamboo. T is Dots (B)? No:\n  - W: Characters\n  - T: Bamboos\n  - B: Dots (Bing)\n  So I have:\n  - W: Characters (W3, W4\u00d73)\n  - B: Dots (B4,B5,B6)\n  - F: Winds\n  \u2192 So I have W (Characters), B (Dots), and honors \u2192 missing **T (Bamboos)** \u2192 **One Voided Suit (1 point)**\n\nWait \u2014 but the across player has T3,T9; previous has W7,W8,W9; next has B-chows.\n\nBut for me: I have no T (Bamboos) tiles in hand or melds \u2192 correct, I have **only W (Characters), B (Dots), and Honors** \u2192 two suited suits \u2192 so **not** one voided suit \u2192 I have **two suits present** \u2192 one voided suit means exactly one suit missing \u2192 yes, **T (Bamboos)** is missing \u2192 so **One Voided Suit (1 point)** applies.\n\nBut wait: \u201cOne Voided Suit\u201d = hand contains tiles from exactly two of the three suits \u2192 yes, I have W and B \u2192 missing T \u2192 valid.\n\nNow, back to decision.\n\nIf I discard **B6**, I keep W3, wait for another W3 to pair.\n\nIf I discard **W3**, I keep B6, wait for another B6.\n\nWhich tile is safer to wait on?\n\nLook at **discards**:\n\n- Has W3 been discarded? Next player played W3 \u2192 yes, one W3 discarded.\n- Total W3: 4 copies\n  - One discarded by next player\n  - One in my hand\n  - So 2 remain \u2192 matches unshown (W3: 2)\n- B6: none discarded visibly \u2192 I have one, next player used one in [B6,B7,B8], so two used, two left \u2192 unshown says B6: 2 \u2192 ok.\n\nNow, who might win on what?\n\nBut more importantly: **am I close to any high-scoring pattern?**\n\nCurrent fan count estimate:\n- Melded Hand: 6 (since all melds formed via other players' discards, and I\u2019ll win by claiming a tile)\n- Prevalent Wind (F1 pung): 2\n- Seat Wind (F1 pung): 2\n- One Voided Suit: 1\n- Pung of Honors (F1, F3, W4 not honor) \u2192 F1 and F3 are wind honors \u2192 each gives 2 points? No:\n  - \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\": 1 point per such pung \u2192 I have:\n    - F1: wind \u2192 1 point\n    - F3: wind \u2192 1 point\n    - W4: not terminal or honor \u2192 no\n    - [B4,B5,B6]: not\n  \u2192 So 2 points from two honor pungs\n- But \u201cDragon Pung\u201d is separate \u2014 I have no dragon melds \u2192 J1,J2,J3 not in my melds\n- I have a kong of F3 \u2192 \u201cMelded Kong\u201d (exposed kong) \u2192 1 point? Wait:\n  - \u201cMelded Kong\u201d: 1 point \u2014 yes, I have one (F3 kong was Peng then BuGang)\n  - Also, \u201cTwo Melded Kongs\u201d? No, only one kong\n- \u201cTwo Dragon Pungs\u201d? No\n- \u201cAll Pungs\u201d? No, I have a chow\n\nTotal estimated fans:\n- Melded Hand: 6\n- Prevalent Wind: 2\n- Seat Wind: 2\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors (x2: F1, F3): 1+1 = 2\n- Melded Kong: 1\n- One Voided Suit: 1\n\u2192 Total: 6+2+2+2+1+1 = **14 fans** \u2192 well above 8.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **Melded Hand** requires that **all melds and the pair** are completed using opponent\u2019s tiles. Currently:\n- [F1,F1,F1]: Peng \u2192 from opponent \u2192 ok\n- [F3,F3,F3,F3]: started as Peng, then BuGang \u2192 ok\n- [W4,W4,W4]: Peng \u2192 from opponent \u2192 ok\n- [B4,B5,B6]: Chi \u2192 from previous player \u2192 ok\n- Pair: must be formed by stealing the winning tile.\n\nSo if I win by claiming a W3 or B6 discarded by someone, then **Melded Hand (6)** applies.\n\nIf I draw it myself, then not Melded Hand, but possibly **Self Drawn (1)** instead.\n\nBut since I\u2019m choosing between discarding B6 or W3, and waiting to claim the other, **Melded Hand is achievable**.\n\nNow, which discard is better?\n\nCompare safety and likelihood.\n\nDiscard **B6**:\n- Leaves me waiting on W3 (need one more W3)\n- W3: 2 left\n- But W3 is in Character suit, and previous player just completed [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 they may not have W3\n- Next player discarded W3 earlier \u2192 they might be discarding safely\n- Across player hasn\u2019t shown much W\n- But W3 is not terminal, so less dangerous\n\nDiscard **W3**:\n- Leaves me waiting on B6\n- B6 is Dots, number 6 \u2014 simple, not terminal\n- Across player has [B7,B8,B9], next has [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 so they may be dot-heavy\n- But B6 is not critical for them now \u2014 their chows are already made\n\nBoth are relatively safe.\n\nBut consider: if I discard **W3**, I keep B6. Is B6 more useful? No \u2014 I already have a chow with it.\n\nSimilarly, W3 is standalone.\n\nBut note: **W4 is completely used up (4 copies)** \u2014 I have the pung, so no one else can use W4.\n\nAlso, **W5, W8, W9 are all gone** \u2014 so no one can make chows involving those easily.\n\nNow, **potential danger**: Could someone win on a W3?\n\nUnlikely \u2014 W3 is not part of any obvious straight flush.\n\nBut let\u2019s see: a Pure Straight in W would need W1-9, but W5=0, so impossible.\n\nSimilarly, Full Flush in W? Possible, but W5 gone \u2192 no.\n\nSo W3 is safe to discard.\n\nSame for B6: B3=0, B5=0 \u2192 hard to form sequences.\n\nBut here\u2019s a key point: **I already have a chow in B4,B5,B6** \u2014 so if I discard B6, I break nothing.\n\nBut **B6 is part of a possible shifted chow**? Unlikely.\n\nMore importantly: **which tile am I more likely to complete the pair with?**\n\nStatistically same \u2014 2 of each left.\n\nBut look at **who is discarding what**.\n\nThe previous player (to my right) just played T7 \u2014 they seem to be cleaning up.\n\nNext player (left) last played T7 \u2014 they are likely waiting.\n\nAcross player seems to have a strong hand \u2014 already has three melds.\n\nI should avoid giving anyone an easy win.\n\nBut neither B6 nor W3 is a common winning tile.\n\nNow, **strategic consideration**: I have a kong on F3 (West), but F3 is already gone (0 unshown) \u2014 so safe.\n\nBut I want to win soon.\n\nSince both waits are equal in value and safety, I should choose based on **tile utility**.\n\nBut no difference.\n\nWait \u2014 **scoring nuance**:\n\nIf I keep **W3**, and later draw or claim another W3, I get a pair of W3 \u2014 which is a simple tile (3-dot) \u2192 no bonus.\n\nSame for B6 \u2014 simple.\n\nBut W3 is in Characters, B6 in Dots \u2014 no difference.\n\nAnother idea: **could I have a chance at Nine Gates or something?** No \u2014 too many exposed melds.\n\nNo special patterns.\n\nSo base decision: both moves are nearly equal.\n\nBut let\u2019s check **valid actions**: [Play B6; Play W3]\n\nI must pick one.\n\nNow, recall: I performed **BuGang F3** earlier \u2014 that means I added a tile to a pung to make a kong. After that, I drew a replacement tile \u2014 which was probably W3 or B6.\n\nThen I\u2019ve been discarding.\n\nNow, the **Out With Replacement Tile** (8 points) applies if I win immediately after drawing from a kong.\n\nBut I didn\u2019t win then \u2014 I discarded \u2014 so missed that chance.\n\nNo longer relevant.\n\nBack to choice.\n\nPerhaps **discard the tile that is less likely to help opponents**.\n\nBut both are safe.\n\nAlternatively, **keep the tile that could form part of a different pair if drawn?** No \u2014 I can only hold one tile.\n\nAfter discard, I\u2019ll have one tile, wait for its duplicate.\n\nBut here\u2019s a subtle point: **if I discard B6**, I keep W3. W3 is in the same suit as my W4 pung. Could that matter? Not really.\n\nBut W3 and W4 are adjacent \u2014 but I can\u2019t form a chow with W3 and W4 alone.\n\nUnless I had W2 or W5 \u2014 but W5=0 \u2192 impossible.\n\nSo no benefit.\n\nSimilarly, B6 and B4/B5 \u2014 but I already used them in a chow.\n\nFinal thought: **frequency of discards**.\n\nLooking at game history:\n\n- W3 was discarded once by next player\n- B6 has never been discarded\n- So B6 might be rarer, thus safer to discard? Or riskier?\n\nActually, since B6 hasn\u2019t been discarded, opponents may still be holding onto it, so **less likely to be discarded again soon**.\n\nWhereas W3 has already been discarded, suggesting it\u2019s safer \u2014 so more likely to appear again.\n\nThus, **W3 is more likely to be discarded by an opponent** than B6.\n\nTherefore, if I discard **B6**, I keep W3, and wait for W3 \u2014 which has already been thrown once \u2192 higher chance it will be thrown again.\n\nWhereas B6 has never been thrown \u2014 maybe someone is saving it.\n\nSo better to **discard B6**, keep W3, wait for W3.\n\nMoreover, next player already threw W3 \u2014 they might throw it again if they have multiple.\n\nCount: next player has only one W3 in history \u2014 so likely only had one.\n\nBut still, W3 has appeared, B6 has not.\n\nIn Mahjong, tiles that have been discarded are often safer because players tend to discard entire sets.\n\nBut here, only one W3 discarded.\n\nStill, **W3 is more exposed** than B6.\n\nThus, **W3 is more likely to be available** for claiming.\n\nTherefore, better to **keep W3** and **discard B6**.\n\nThis way, I wait on a tile that has already surfaced, increasing my chance of completing the pair.\n\nReason: Maximizing win probability by retaining a tile (W3) that has already been discarded once, indicating lower opponent dependency and higher likelihood of re-emergence, compared to B6 which remains unseen and may be held tightly.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
